WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.141
Welcome to section ten, interventional pulmonary procedures.

2
00:00:05.141 --> 00:00:23.860
The lesson seven A deck and Hess call bronchoscopy the most commonly used invasive procedure in pulmonary medicine, and it is both diagnostic and therapeutic; Hess adds that about five hundred thousand are done each year in the United States.

3
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:45.750
In this lesson we will cover the diagnostic-versus-therapeutic split, indications and contraindications, flexible versus rigid scopes, sedation and the reversal agents, monitoring, the sampling techniques, the chapter nine key points, complications, and preparation and reprocessing.

4
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:58.428
The RCP 105 deck puts it simply: diagnostic means find out what is wrong, or look; therapeutic means fix a problem, or treat.

5
00:00:58.428 --> 00:01:09.780
Diagnostic examples are airway inspection, bronchoalveolar lavage, washing, and biopsy, used to identify tumors or infections.

6
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:25.906
Therapeutic examples are removing mucus plugs or foreign bodies, controlling bleeding, and relieving airway obstruction by dilation, stenting, or debulking an endobronchial tumor.

7
00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:38.969
The RCP 105 deck says that if a question asks which procedure is both diagnostic and therapeutic, fiberoptic bronchoscopy is an excellent answer.

8
00:01:38.969 --> 00:01:56.267
Hess adds a safety rule: during any airway heat therapy, such as laser, electrocautery, or argon plasma coagulation, reduce FiO2 to forty percent or less so flammable components do not ignite.

9
00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:13.837
The lesson seven A deck lists nine indications: atelectasis, hemoptysis, cough, suspected malignancy, infection, foreign body aspiration, interstitial lung disease, lung transplant, and trauma.

10
00:02:13.837 --> 00:02:31.265
For atelectasis, bronchoscopy is most valuable when a bronchus is obstructed, but try noninvasive airway clearance first, such as bronchodilators when indicated, chest physiotherapy, coughing, and suctioning.

11
00:02:31.265 --> 00:02:39.643
For hemoptysis, it identifies the cause, localizes the bleeding, and looks for endobronchial cancer.

12
00:02:39.643 --> 00:02:51.960
Foreign body aspiration is common in children; the classic triad is sudden paroxysmal coughing, wheezing, and diminished breath sounds on one side.

13
00:02:51.960 --> 00:03:00.757
Rigid bronchoscopy is often preferred for large objects and remains the instrument of choice in children.

14
00:03:00.757 --> 00:03:16.342
Hess adds that transbronchial biopsy is the gold standard for acute transplant rejection, and bronchoscopy remains the standard for diagnosing tracheobronchial injury after blunt trauma.

15
00:03:17.767 --> 00:03:24.702
Three concerns dominate: oxygenation, hemodynamic stability, and bleeding risk.

16
00:03:24.702 --> 00:03:49.022
The AARC criteria in the RCP 203 deck say to recommend against bronchoscopy if the patient cannot be adequately oxygenated, has a bleeding disorder that cannot be corrected, has severe obstructive airway disease, has severe refractory hypoxemia, or is hemodynamically unstable.

17
00:03:49.022 --> 00:04:26.773
Hess adds numbers: a PaO2 under seventy millimeters of mercury despite supplemental oxygen is generally considered a contraindication; the British Thoracic Society, quoted by Hess, says to defer at least six weeks after a myocardial infarction; avoid bronchoscopy in status asthmaticus; and platelets should be above fifty thousand for transbronchial biopsy, with a minimum of twenty to fifty thousand for fiberoptic bronchoscopy.

18
00:04:26.773 --> 00:04:35.992
Normal PT is eleven to thirteen point five seconds, and normal PTT is twenty-five to thirty-five seconds.

19
00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:59.953
The flexible fiberoptic or video scope has a soft shaft three point five to six millimeters wide, and its tip rotates up to two hundred ten degrees; the RCP 105 deck instead says it bends about one hundred eighty degrees up and one hundred thirty down, but use two hundred ten for this topic.

20
00:04:59.953 --> 00:05:10.534
It usually goes in through the nose under local anesthesia with sedation and reaches the smaller, distal airways for biopsies and lavage.

21
00:05:10.534 --> 00:05:27.139
The rigid scope is a straight stainless-steel tube, nine to thirteen millimeters wide in adults, placed through the mouth with the neck hyperextended under general anesthesia, and it gives definitive airway control.

22
00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:37.103
Rigid is chosen for severe central obstruction, large foreign bodies, significant bleeding, silicone stents, and laser resection.

23
00:05:37.103 --> 00:05:42.200
It is also the instrument of choice for a foreign body in a child.

24
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.147
Three drug names, and two antidotes, because one of them has none.

25
00:05:49.147 --> 00:06:07.719
Midazolam, trade name Versed, is a benzodiazepine giving sedation, anxiolysis, and amnesia, but no pain relief; it is reversed by flumazenil, which can precipitate seizures, so it is not given routinely to speed recovery.

26
00:06:07.719 --> 00:06:16.627
Fentanyl, trade name Sublimaze, is an opioid for analgesia and is reversed by naloxone, trade name Narcan.

27
00:06:16.627 --> 00:06:24.275
Fentanyl is often combined with midazolam, which raises the risk of respiratory depression.

28
00:06:24.275 --> 00:06:38.225
Propofol, trade name Diprivan, is a sedative-hypnotic with rapid onset; it causes respiratory depression and hypotension, has no analgesia, and has no reversal agent.

29
00:06:39.633 --> 00:06:50.428
Hess says lidocaine is the most commonly used topical agent because of its favorable safety profile and short half-life.

30
00:06:50.428 --> 00:06:59.333
The RCP 104 deck adds that it blocks sodium channels, and it suppresses cough and numbs the mucosa.

31
00:06:59.333 --> 00:07:01.762
The doses differ by source.

32
00:07:01.762 --> 00:07:22.092
The RCP 105 deck gives five to ten milliliters of a one to four percent solution, by aerosol or instilled down the working channel starting at the vocal cords; Hess gives two to three milliliter aliquots of one to two percent.

33
00:07:22.092 --> 00:07:25.780
Use the RCP 105 deck's dose on this exam.

34
00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:31.717
Hess caps the total dose at five to seven milligrams per kilogram.

35
00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:39.902
Atropine, given intramuscularly, reduces vagal response, oral secretions, and bronchospasm.

36
00:07:39.902 --> 00:07:47.908
Hess notes many centers have dropped it, but for what atropine does, use the deck answer.

37
00:07:49.333 --> 00:07:50.653
Pause and think.

38
00:07:50.653 --> 00:08:01.048
A patient has received midazolam and fentanyl for bronchoscopy, and now the breathing slows and the patient is hard to arouse.

39
00:08:01.048 --> 00:08:05.421
Which reversal agent matches each of those two drugs?

40
00:08:05.421 --> 00:08:10.206
What caution comes with the benzodiazepine reversal agent?

41
00:08:10.206 --> 00:08:17.383
And if the sedative had been propofol instead, what reversal agent would you reach for?

42
00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:24.300
[6-second practice pause]

43
00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:31.209
Fentanyl is the opioid, so it is reversed by naloxone, trade name Narcan.

44
00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:45.169
Midazolam is the benzodiazepine, so it is reversed by flumazenil, but flumazenil can precipitate seizures, so it is not given routinely just to speed recovery.

45
00:08:45.169 --> 00:08:56.583
Propofol is neither an opioid nor a benzodiazepine, and it has no reversal agent, so you support the airway and breathing instead.

46
00:08:56.583 --> 00:09:07.646
Hess warns that benzodiazepines and opioids together potentiate respiratory depression, so keep naloxone and flumazenil ready.

47
00:09:07.646 --> 00:09:19.850
The deck's memory trick: fentanyl, pain, opioid, naloxone; Versed, sedation and amnesia, benzo, flumazenil; propofol, neither, no reversal.

48
00:09:21.267 --> 00:09:36.992
During the procedure, the RT sets up continuous SpO2, ECG and heart rate, blood pressure, and respiratory rate monitoring, plus capnography when indicated by the sedation protocol.

49
00:09:36.992 --> 00:09:47.039
Transient hypoxemia occurs in up to thirty-five percent of patients and is often relieved with supplemental oxygen.

50
00:09:47.039 --> 00:10:05.735
The RCP 105 deck allows up to six liters per minute for spontaneously breathing patients without an artificial airway; Hess gives two to three liters per minute to everyone, but use the RCP 105 number on this exam.

51
00:10:05.735 --> 00:10:24.605
On a ventilator, the scope in the tube raises airway resistance, so peak pressure may rise, delivered tidal volume may fall, FiO2 may be raised, often to one hundred percent, and alarms may need temporary adjustment.

52
00:10:24.605 --> 00:10:33.167
Afterward, monitor vital signs at least two hours or until stable, with continuous pulse oximetry.

53
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:47.742
COPD is the umbrella diagnosis: progressive, irreversible airflow limitation, confirmed by a post-bronchodilator FEV1 over FVC below zero point seven.

54
00:10:47.742 --> 00:11:01.847
Emphysema is its alveolar-destruction half; the other half, chronic bronchitis, is a productive cough for at least three months in each of two consecutive years.

55
00:11:01.847 --> 00:11:17.441
A scabbard trachea can be seen in emphysema, and bronchoscopically placed one-way valves let trapped air out in selected advanced cases, with pneumothorax the major complication.

56
00:11:17.441 --> 00:11:26.641
GERD is reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus and one of the most common causes of chronic cough.

57
00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:37.241
Hess calls bronchoscopy the procedure most commonly used to diagnose lung cancer, with biopsy, and EBUS-TBNA for staging.

58
00:11:37.241 --> 00:11:44.075
The study guide notes the deck never explains why these four are grouped here.

59
00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:49.411
Four sampling techniques, plus needle aspiration.

60
00:11:49.411 --> 00:12:08.090
Bronchoalveolar lavage wedges the scope in a segmental or subsegmental bronchus, then instills and suctions back saline to sample the distal airways and alveoli, for pneumonia, opportunistic infections, and some interstitial diseases.

61
00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:17.189
Hess keeps the total at two hundred milliliters or less and suction under one hundred twenty centimeters of water.

62
00:12:17.189 --> 00:12:22.857
Washing instills saline without wedging and samples the larger airways.

63
00:12:22.857 --> 00:12:33.393
The standard brush collects surface cells for cytology; the protected brush collects organisms with less upper-airway contamination.

64
00:12:33.393 --> 00:12:43.850
Endobronchial biopsy uses forceps on a visible airway lesion; transbronchial lung biopsy samples parenchyma beyond the airway wall.

65
00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:57.100
Transbronchial needle aspiration passes a needle through the wall to sample beyond the airway, including lymph nodes, guided in real time by endobronchial ultrasound.

66
00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:06.894
Hess chapter nine key points: bronchoscopy is safe and effective, and both diagnostic and therapeutic.

67
00:13:06.894 --> 00:13:11.338
The ideal fiberoptic patient is awake and cooperative.

68
00:13:11.338 --> 00:13:22.776
The deck's version of key point nine says rigid bronchoscopy is mainly for complex therapeutic work and typically needs general anesthesia.

69
00:13:22.776 --> 00:13:30.923
Stents are self-expandable metal, silicone, or hybrid, and one-way valves treat advanced emphysema.

70
00:13:30.923 --> 00:13:53.389
Hess calls pleural fluid exudative if the pleural-to-serum protein ratio is at least zero point five, or the LDH ratio is at least zero point six; the deck instead teaches transudate as a pressure problem and exudate as inflammation, infection, or malignancy, so know both.

71
00:13:53.389 --> 00:14:04.087
The deck recommends ultrasound guidance for thoracentesis, and Hess considers removing fifteen hundred milliliters at a time safe.

72
00:14:04.087 --> 00:14:14.950
A pH under seven point two zero with glucose under sixty milligrams per deciliter may indicate a complicated parapneumonic effusion.

73
00:14:16.367 --> 00:14:17.707
Pause and think.

74
00:14:17.707 --> 00:14:27.847
During a routine flexible bronchoscopy, which complication does the RCP 105 deck call the one you are most likely to see?

75
00:14:27.847 --> 00:14:38.742
And for a spontaneously breathing patient without an artificial airway, how many liters per minute of oxygen does that deck allow?

76
00:14:39.667 --> 00:14:45.667
[6-second practice pause]

77
00:14:46.167 --> 00:14:54.618
Hypoxemia is the complication you are most likely to see, in up to thirty-five percent of patients.

78
00:14:54.618 --> 00:15:03.496
If the saturation falls, the RT raises the oxygen, tells the physician, and the procedure may be paused.

79
00:15:03.496 --> 00:15:18.520
The RCP 105 deck allows oxygen up to six liters per minute for a spontaneously breathing patient without an artificial airway; Hess says two to three, but use six on this exam.

80
00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:31.410
Pneumothorax occurs in one to six percent, mainly after transbronchial biopsy; suspect it with sudden dyspnea, chest pain, and decreased breath sounds.

81
00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:47.630
Significant hemorrhage, more than fifty milliliters, occurs in two to nine percent; airway and oxygenation come first, then iced saline, epinephrine, or thrombin through the working channel.

82
00:15:47.630 --> 00:16:00.690
Epinephrine strengths differ: the RCP 105 slide lists one to one thousand, its notes dilute to one to ten thousand, and Hess uses one to twenty thousand.

83
00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:05.642
If asked what the slide lists, answer one to one thousand.

84
00:16:07.067 --> 00:16:20.783
Before the procedure, the physician obtains consent before sedatives are given, and the RT reviews vital signs, oxygenation, allergies, and bleeding risks.

85
00:16:20.783 --> 00:16:31.934
The deck says NPO, typically six to eight hours; Hess describes fasting after midnight, but use the deck's six to eight hours.

86
00:16:31.934 --> 00:16:48.394
Afterward, monitor vital signs at least two hours or until stable, maintain SpO2 with oxygen, and withhold food or drink until the gag reflex returns, because lidocaine numbs the throat.

87
00:16:48.394 --> 00:16:52.199
Hess adds no driving for twenty-four hours.

88
00:16:52.199 --> 00:16:57.952
Stridor gets racemic epinephrine; wheezing gets a bronchodilator.

89
00:16:57.952 --> 00:17:02.642
The RCP 203 deck calls the bronchoscope semicritical.

90
00:17:02.642 --> 00:17:12.022
The RCP 105 deck says sterilize it, but for an NBRC-style question, high-level disinfection is acceptable.

91
00:17:12.022 --> 00:17:22.553
Glutaraldehyde gives high-level disinfection in twenty minutes at room temperature; true sterilization takes ten hours.

92
00:17:22.553 --> 00:17:27.067
Biopsy forceps are critical and must be sterilized.

93
00:17:28.467 --> 00:17:32.320
Bronchoscopy is diagnostic and therapeutic.

94
00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:40.922
Flexible for routine diagnostic work, rigid for central airway control and large foreign bodies.

95
00:17:40.922 --> 00:17:54.005
Sedate with midazolam, fentanyl, or propofol; reverse midazolam with flumazenil and fentanyl with naloxone, and remember propofol has no reversal.

96
00:17:54.005 --> 00:18:03.145
Lidocaine is the topical anesthetic, and Hess caps the total at five to seven milligrams per kilogram.

97
00:18:03.145 --> 00:18:15.779
BAL samples the alveolar space, washing samples airways, brushing samples surface cells, biopsy samples tissue, and TBNA samples lymph nodes.

98
00:18:15.779 --> 00:18:25.278
The complication to anticipate is hypoxemia; the complications to watch for are pneumothorax and bleeding.

99
00:18:25.278 --> 00:18:31.192
Keep FiO2 at forty percent or less during any airway heat therapy.
